```java
// throws — declares in signature
public void readFile(String path) throws IOException {
    if (path == null) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("path cannot be null"); // throw — action
    }
    // ... file reading code that may throw IOException
}
```

Declaring `throws` without actually throwing compiles fine. The legitimate reason to do this is **interface/abstract method design**: you declare throws in an interface method so that implementing classes have the flexibility to throw checked exceptions without breaking the interface contract.

```java
// Interface — declares throws for implementor flexibility
public interface DataLoader {
    Data load(int id) throws IOException;
}

// Implementor 1 — throws
class FileLoader implements DataLoader {
    public Data load(int id) throws IOException { /*...*/ }
}

// Implementor 2 — doesn't throw (still valid)
class CacheLoader implements DataLoader {
    public Data load(int id) { return cache.get(id); }
}
```

```java
class Parent {
    void read() throws IOException { }
}
class Child extends Parent {
    // OK: narrower exception
    void read() throws FileNotFoundException { }
    // COMPILE ERROR: broader than IOException
    // void read() throws Exception { }
    // OK: unchecked exception — always allowed
    // void read() throws RuntimeException { }
}
```

```java
// Pattern 1: rethrow same exception
try { riskyMethod(); }
catch (IOException e) {
    log.error("Error", e);
    throw e; // rethrows the same IOException
}

// Pattern 2: wrap and rethrow
try { riskyMethod(); }
catch (IOException e) {
    throw new ServiceException("Failed", e); // wraps with cause
}
```

Problems with `throws Exception`:

1. **No information** — the caller has no idea what kind of failure to expect
2. **Forces catch(Exception e)** — callers end up with overly broad catches
3. **Defeats documentation** — throws is meant to communicate failure modes
4. **Hides bugs** — RuntimeExceptions that should be fixed are swallowed in catch(Exception)

The correct approach is to declare specific exceptions:
```java
// Bad
public User findUser(int id) throws Exception { }

// Good
public User findUser(int id) throws UserNotFoundException, DataAccessException { }
```
